analysis:
Examined docs/developer/test.md base units 1-474 and 475-945, plus the patch. The head changes are the date bump at line 3 and a new 'Add-On Hold' test section at head lines 767-790. That section documents test suites for an add-on hold IPC feature: an AF_UNIX socket, flock, owner-only record, peer policy and a hold.v1 protocol. It also documents serving-loop behavior: a draining heartbeat with busy fields, no provider_drain, 503 draining rejection, a planned reconnect that keeps the hold in register, and ProviderBusyWireTests for heartbeat/register encodings. This is documentation only and changes no security control in the supplied evidence.

The documentation does reveal new local attack surface: a local IPC socket that can unload every model and drain the provider. That fits TB-003 (operator vs. process) and availability; the operator already controls the machine, so impact is mainly self-DoS. The new busy/drain fields on the heartbeat and register wire are relevant to TB-002 and the coordinator contract. The canonical threat model has no entry for this add-on hold IPC or for the busy wire fields. Integration should check whether the implementation PR (in source files not in this batch) updates the threat model for these items:
- peer authentication of non-operator local processes (analogous to T-044 for the fan helper);
- socket permissions;
- whether a hold can suppress revocation or reconnect invalidation (T-053).

The excerpt is truncated after 'The peer check is exercised only neg...'. The rest of the section was not visible, so no conclusion can be drawn from it. No regression is supported by the doc diff alone.
